     CITIZEN'S COALITION SEEKS ORGANIZATIONAL ENDORSEMENTS
     FOR TELECOMMUNICATIONS "PUBLIC INTEREST PRINCIPLES"

     WASHINGTON, DC.  On September 1, 1993, a citizens
coalition, currently operating under the name Telecommunications
Policy Roundtable, sent out its revised "Public Interest
Principles."  The group wants to circulate these principles
widely, and hopes to get organizations to endorse the principles
by September 17.  (The group has also tentatively scheduled a
press conference for October 7.)

     The "Public Interest Principles" were revised by the group
over August, and reflected input received from a wide range of
organizations, as well as the Internet community, via electronic
mail.  Kathryn Montgomery, the co-director of the Center for
Media Education, was responsible for coordinating the drafting
of the principles.

     As noted, the Telecommunications Policy Roundtable is
seeking *organizational* endorsements for its principles.
Organizations willing to sign on should send their permission
to:

     Kathryn Montgomery
     Center for Media Education
     1511 K Street, Suite 518      [Note:  this is a new
     Washington, DC  20005                      address]
     voice:  202/628-2620
     fax:  202/628-2554
     internet:  cme@access.digex.net

     On a related issue, on Tuesday, September 7, 1993, the
Roundtable is meeting in Washington with Larry Irving, the
Assistant Secretary of Commerce for Communications and
Information (and the new head of the National Telecommunications
and Information Administration), to discuss the Clinton/Gore
administration's policy initiatives in telecommunications and
media policy.

     Persons wanting more information about the Roundtable are
