>  If the user is _still around_ and they're not reading their mail in a
>timely fashion, that's a whole new kettle of fish.  I can't think of a generic
>way to handle that.  

I have a simple rule.  If/when one's mailbox hits one megabyte in size,
it is moved into one's home directory, where it now counts against disk
resource quotas.

You could always put /usr/mail (or /var/spool/mail, or whatever) on a
separate partition and invoke quotas on it...

>  IMHO, I'd avoid handing mail at all.  

Indeed; I don't even include email in my backup tapes.  Given the nebulous
legal status of email (private?  subject to "sunshine" laws?  retrievable
via subpoena?), I just don't want to fool with it.  All our users know that
email is not archived.  If they save something (from their mailbox) into a 
regular file in their diskspace, I believe that file would fall under a dif-
ferent set of rules, such as FERPA.  [ Of course, I could be mistaken; more
knowledgeable netters are invited to correct me. ]

--Wes

