BTW, Carl, thanks for posting the full pointer!

>Contrary to popular belief purpose of a university's computing infrastructure
>is not to foster free expression about any and all subjects, but to support the
>teaching and research activities of the university's staff and students. Any
>other use of the infrastructure is tolerated at the discretion of the 
>administration, and is not a right or entitlement.

Of course the administration has the last word about all of this (whether
it's a right or not).

But I beg to differ about the goals of academic computing infrastructure
nowadays. The problem in our case is that the computing infrastructure is
more and more used as communication medium, and as an information access
medium (much the goals that libraries, publications, and the post office
had in the past). That makes it pretty hard to determine what is
appropriate use and what is not. If you accept the information access
and communication idea, then removing newsgroups is censorship (it does
not matter which newsgroup). Censorship is not very compatible with my
idea of an academic institution (your milage may vary).

Besides, if we follow your direction, the university should probably first
fine people who post personal "for sale" ads for washing machines and
exercise bikes. I do not know of cases where the "for sale" newsgroups
were removed :-)

>It would be different if a commercial system were trying to restrict what 
>data its customers stored or transmitted. In that case going beyond the
>legal restrictions could be called excessive.

Ridiculous (although I'm sure it depends on the country). In this case
much would be covered today by contract law (at least in the US, and the way
I understand it): If the system promised all the newsgroups, then it would
have to do just that. If it did not, explicitely or implicitely, then it
does not have to. The case of restricting contents of private email
is unclear to me in the US, a "common carrier" system such as the phone
company clearly could not, but a BBS, or internet node, is in the
gray area. Some BBSes clearly state that the sysop is reviewing all material
going through, reserves the right to remove any and all such material,
and that there is no such thing as private email on their machine....

>And what are you trying to say by putting academic institutions into quotes?

Only that in several cases, the newsgroups seem to be removed by rank and
file system administrators, without specific instructions from the school's
administration, or with instructions from one high ranking official, without
going through normal decision making processes. In these cases, I would
contend, the decision is that of some individual, or small group (as in
Information Services Support Group for example), rather than the decision
of the university.

Often the decision by the individual can be hasty (but dreadfully efficient),
whereas the academic decision per se would be a drawn out process
involving more administrators, policy makers, trustees, professors,
students. The initial decision could be a mere reaction to a press
article, whereas the second (maybe :-?) reflects more the values of
the academic institution. Still, if the initial decision targets a
student instead of a newsgroup, some damage can be done by the time
the second decision arrives.

Same problem exactly with law enforcement agencies and the court system, you
may have rights, but if your professional computer systems have been
recklessly confisquated for months, you are in trouble, rights or no rights.
The well known example in this case is Steve Jackson Games versus the US
Secret Service (or FBI or whatever) (again see the EFF archives). In this
case, even though common sense eventually prevails in court, the damage to
a business has been done, and is not repaired.

Pierre.
