From: pgut1@cs.aukuni.ac.nz (Peter Gutmann)
Subject: Re: [CHE] Highlights from Chronicle of Higher Ed.
Date: Thu, 16 Sep 1993 12:29:07 GMT
Message-ID: <1993Sep16.122907.9901@cs.aukuni.ac.nz>

>>There was also a short article in CAF-News a month ago. Does anyone
>>have more details about what has happened since the bans?
>
>There has been no sign of any change in policy on the part of U of C. An 
>extremely draconian anti-porn law has also been passed by the NZ government
>(anticipation of this law was undoubtedly one of the motivations for U of 
>C's action).  Among other stupidities, this law regards possession in any 
>form (including electronic) of certain types of pornography (e.g. child 
>porn) as indefensible.  As we all know, such things frequently appear in 
>inappropriate newsgroups, so U of C are certain to be in `possession' of 
>offensive material sooner or later, and they have of course seriously 
>undermined the possibility of pleading the impracticality of monitoring 
>incoming news postings by their actions in censoring some newsgroups.

In case anyone's interested, here are a few relevant sections of the new law 
(complete with my totally unbiased:-) comments), from a discussion in 
nz.general a few weeks ago...

>  "Publication" means--
>[...]
>  (c) Any paper or other thing--
>[...]
>    (ii) On which is recorded or stored any information that, by the use of
>         any computer or other electronic device, is capable of being 
>         reproduced or shown as any word, statement, sign, or representation.

This shows the (typical) utter cluelessness of the people who drafted the 
law.  There is no "normal use" clause here - it's possible to interpret data 
in any way you choose if you want to.  There's no way that a jury consisting 
of people who can't set the timers on their VCR's are going to be able to 
differentiate between decoding a JPEG file and "decoding" an Excel 
spreadsheet to get, in both cases, a politically-incorrect image.

>    3. Meaning of "objectionable"--(1) For the purposes of this Act, 
>  a publication is objectionable if it describes, depicts, expresses, 
>  or otherwise deals with matters of sex, horror, crime, cruelty, or 
>  violence in such a manner that the availability of the publication 
>  is likely to be injurious to the public good. 
>
>  [long list of policitically incorrect topics covering virtually everything
>   imaginable]
>
>    4. Whether publication objectionable a matter of expert judgement--
>  The question whether or not a publication is objectionable is a matter 
>  for the expert judgement of the person or body authorised or required, 
>  by or pursuant to this Act, to determine it, and evidence as to, or 
>  proof of, any of the matters or particulars that the person or body is 
>  required to consider in determining that question is not essential to 
>  its determination. 

This means that even if the "person or authorised body" can't provide any 
proof or evidence showing that a "publication" is illegal under the act, 
they can still rule that it is "injurious to the public good".  It basically 
allows whoever gets to make the judgement to enfore their morals on everyone 
else.

And now the really scary part:

>Section 121. Offense to possess objectionable publication---
>
>(1) Subject to subsections 4 & 5 of this section, every person commits an 
>    offense against the Act who, without lawful authority or excuse, has in 
>    that person's possession an objectionable publication.
>(2) Every person who commits an offense against subsection 1 of 
>    this section is liable to a fine not exceeding, ---
>        (a) In the case on an individual, $2000:
>        (b) In the case of a body corporate, $5000.
>        [also jail terms which are listed somewhere else]
>(3) It shall be *no* defence to a charge under subsection 1 of this section 
>    that the defendant had no knowledge or no reasonable cause to believe 
>    that the publication to which the charge relates was objectionable.

Note that (3) provides for, in effect, imprisonment without trial.  Once any 
sort of non-PC material is in your hands, you can't defend yourself.  In 
effect you go from arrest straight to sentencing without any cute niceties 
like a trial by jury. 

One person commented:

>Fortunately there should be ample scope for this act to destroy itself. eg. 
>the bible and some feminist literature (the "aren't men awful" type) a 
>clearly and *unquestionably* "objectionable" under this act.
>
>It is our legal duty to surrender copies of these under the pending amnesty
>or perhaps burn them straight off.
>
>Bibles can be found in churches and in the offices and homes of our christian
>friends, relatives and colleagues.  Help them to avoid the wrath of the law 
>destroying or posting their bible in to the amnesty for them.
>
>Feminist literature can be found in libraries, and I presume, offices of
>feminist studies departments.  However, unlike the bible, each volume would 
>to be read to check whether it was unquestionably objectionable.  Borderline
>cases could be submitted to the new authority for a decision.

Unfortunately the feminists and bible-bashers (who, according to a civil- 
liberties group which made submissions on the act, were the main force 
behind it) have conveniently excluded any of their own material from being 
offensive.

Knowing what's in the new law makes the Canterbury Uni position a bit more 
understandable (although I still disagree with it completely).  While I 
don't want to turn this into an imminent death of the net predicted lament, 
the internet is a rather fragile institution over here.  There's just one 
main backbone going to universities and a few companies and 
govt.departments, and there's no official funding for it - it's all paid for 
by universities and the like from computer centre budgets or whatever.  All 
it takes is something like the alt.tasteless crossposts to rec.pets.cats a 
while back followed by a complaint from a self-appointed guardian of public 
morals for an administrator to panic and take drastic self-censorship 
steps.  At $5K fine per non-PC posting a single days news can eat the entire 
years network access budget, and many administrators would rather have a 
highly limited feed than none at all (and a criminal record to boot).

The NZ Internal Affairs dept. has been approached over this (for example 
some people suggested supplying them with a newsfeed in hardcopy format and 
creating a two-year backlog at the end of the first day), but their comment 
was that they basically weren't interested in checking everything (of course 
you're still guilty if you get caught with anything "illegal").  However 
this was before the new law came into effect, I don't know what their 
position is now.

There is, however, one glimmer of hope:  One comment I made during the 
discussion was:

>>(3) Without limiting the power of the Classification Office to classify a 
>>    publication as a restricted publication, a publication that would 
>>    otherwise be classified as objectionable may be classified as a 
>>    restricted publication in order that the publication may be made 
>>    available to particular persons or classes of persons for educational, 
>>    scientific, literary, artistic, or technical purposes.
>
>Hmm, "technical purposes":  "Yes your honour, I keep 2GB of white noise on 
>tapes for use in testing the sound quality of my speakers.  Encryption? 
>What's that?" :-)

As I mentioned before since the people who wrote the act are obviously 
utterly clueless when it comes to computers, the act says absolutely nothing 
about encryption.  The only provision is that the soundtrack of a film may 
have to be translated into English, but nothing else.  In fact if you had 
the resources and without that ^$%^&@! automatically-guilty clause you could 
probably bog any court case down indefinitely with arguments about 
representation of information and use various information theory and 
philosophy-based arguments to prove that either the law or their case as it 
pertains to computer data is a load of rubbish.

However all this does is provide privacy for personal data and 
communications - it still doesn't help mitigate the threat of possible news 
censorship... the only alternative is for anyone who wants an unlimited 
newsfeed to bring it in in encrypted form from the US, which is 
prohibitively expensive.

Oh yes, I don't speak for the Uni of Auckland, and YMMV on the above 
comments. 

Peter.
