I believe that this situation actually ties in with several other
threads discussed within Usenet over the last few years.  I'd
like to make an attempt to tie them together.  Bear with me...

John Franks <john@math.nwu.edu> wrote:
>A sysadmin at Oregon State had a gopher server with data consisting
>of thousands of files whose names were digit strings.  It was made
>known to people who would have interest in the data, but not 
>advertised or "registered" with the gopher people at Minnesota.
>The sysadmin had no objection to people on the net accessing his
>data if they were interested in it.

Apparently, the admin used a "generically public" service to
provide an somewhat local resource.  [ Comparison: the infamous
SRI anonymous FTP discussion. ]  

The fact that the admin did not register his gopher with the 
"authority" for the service is indicative of his desire to limit 
its use.

>Several (I think at least three) automated gopher indexers (e.g.
>veronica) found his gopher and started indexing it.  

Here we find outside agencies which, for whatever reason, publi-
cized the existence of this service.  [ Comparison: if my memory
is correct, archie servers used to go out and "find" anonymous ftp 
archives and add them to its database. ]

>He contacted indexers and asked them to stop.  

Apparently, the admin took steps to prevent this activity.  This
is nothing more than the local admin asserting his site's wishes
for a given resource.  [ Comparison: FTP sites which request that
you limit downloads to a particular time of day. ]

>According to the sysadmin they did not do so in a
>timely fashion (on the order of a week, I think).  It is possible
>they were not able to do so quickly, but the sysadmin seemed to
>believe that at least some of the indexers were uncooperative.

I can believe this.  [ Comparison: publication of material from
the net, without the courtesy of requesting permission, in com-
mercial texts.  I was involved in this through the "Publically
Accessible Mailing Lists" document; details available via email,
if you're interested. ]

>The sysadmin posted an angry message to the group
>comp.infosystems.gopher expressing his feelings about gopher indexers.
>He also made the argument that indexers had no right to use his CPU
>without his permission.  

This decision (advertising a gopher) should be left to each site.
No third party should have the right to arbitrarily decide to ad-
vertise it "on their behalf."  The consequences in this case (CPU
hammering) seem to have caused a serious impact on the site in 
question; while other cases may be less intrusive, the act itself
is not justified by any "lack of impact."  [ Comparison: the group
of students that just decided to probe hundreds/thousands of systems
for various security holes (with announcement after the fact) some 
years ago, setting off alarms at security-conscious sites all over 
the place. ]

>A number of people jumped on him (quite
>unfairly in my opinion) saying he should not be trying to hoard his
>data.  

The existence of a given service or capability does not imply any
"right of access."  [ Comparison: any discussion of newsgroup
selection, notably the "full feed or nothing" proponents.  You could
also compare this to the "resource quotas" discussions. ]

>He responded that he had no objection to people accessing his
>data, but he wanted the indexers to stop because 1) they were
>continuing to impede his work and 2) the indexes produced (being only
>numeric strings) were useless.  

This seems logical to me. 

>The sysadmin made several apparently
>unsuccessful efforts to block indexing including moving his server to
>a new port on the same machine.  One of the indexers who had stopped
>indexing found the new port and started indexing again in the belief
>that this was a "new server".  

This seems to imply that automated "find the gophers" programs will
automatically probe networked systems in search of their quarry.  If
this is the case, I must say that I find such probing distasteful.
If I wish to advertise a gopher server to the world, I'll register it.
[ Comparison: archie, again... ]

>The sysadmin was clearly frustrated
>could perhaps be characterized as intemperate in his posts.

I would probably be "intemperate" as well -- especially if I had al-
ready made my wishes/rationales known to those responsible for the
activity in question.

>Responding posters made the argument that having a gopher server
>implicitly gave permission to anyone to use his CPU in whatever way
>gopher supported.  

This is, in my mind, completely incorrect.  Does running an SMTP
server give implicit permission for outsiders (or, for that mat-
ter, insiders) to connect to it manually and forge email?

>Others made the argument that permission was
>implicit in the registration of his gopher server with the Univ of
>Minn.  The sysadmin responded that he had *not* registered it, that
>one of the indexers had "registered it for him".

Here, again, we see third parties making decisions on behalf of a
site, without consultation.  This is arrogant in the extreme.  

>This discussion has lead to a mechanism, supported by at least the 
>Veronica indexers, by which a gopher maintainer can mark a directory
>(or the whole server) not to be indexed.

A better solution would be to ask via email, BEFORE indexing, if the
site wishes to be included in the index.  I would prefer to see the
"is it public?" question default to NO.  If the default is to allow
indexing, I would hope that the gopher documentation makes the "no
indexing" procedure quite clear.

