In article <1993Sep16.000253.1293@cs.uwp.edu>, datta@cs.uwp.edu (David Datta)
says:
>
>With the definition of harassment including unsolicited e-mail, this
>type of case can most likely be included under harassment in most
>student disciplinary procedures.
>
     The difficulty is that such a definition of harassment entails
that a *host* of cases fall under the definition of harassment,
including plenty that appear to be far from harassment as the term
is normally used in English.

     For example:  Suppose I used the "Reply" rather than the
"Followup" function after reading your post.

     Suppose I send a note to Carl Kadie asking for some information
on ALA policies and the net.

     Suppose I mistype the address when I write to Carl, and it goes
to someone else.

     One might argue that the first of these isn't unsolicited.  That
might be true in this case, but the point isn't generalizable over
similar situations.  A post may be construed as an invitation to
followup, but absent other indicators, replies are "unsolicited."

     The second of these is also unsolicited, but it may not be
unwanted.  Neither it nor the first should be banned.

     The third is not only unsolicited and unwanted, it would be
clear to anyone that it was unwanted.  It shouldn't be banned.

     It seems to me that, and this goes beyond
Carl's suggested amendment, an acceptable policy must distinguish
between intentional and other acts.  Though some who inhabit these
parts may advise you to write the vague rule you want and to use
common sense in applying it, that way lies danger.  Vague rules,
and rules that are designed to extend beyond the scope meant to be
enforced, guarantee that authorities must practice selective
enforcement.  This sets the enforcers up for charges that they act
(1) arbitrarily, (2) capriciously, (3) in a discriminatory
fashion, and it probably undermines users' respect for the
complete set of rules (since the authorities clearly don't
respect them all equally).

